  approaches and a written spec before any code, rather than the few-sentences design I offered.

  Your approval of the return-value shape carries forward: [38;5;153mlogin()[39m learns the id from the API response rather than
  taking it as a parameter. That's settled. What's now open is where it lives afterward.

  First question, and it's the one that governs the rest.

  The fork: whether this id is [1mobservability data[0m or [1msession state[0m. Both "persist across the app," but they are
  different systems with different risk.

  - [1mTracking-only[0m — the id exists to attribute events ("who did this") to a user in logs/analytics. It's
    non-authoritative: if it's missing or tampered with, you log the wrong name and nothing else breaks. Storage can be
    simple and client-side. Cheap to change later.
  - [1mSession state[0m — the app [3mbelieves[0m this id and acts on it: showing user-specific UI, deciding what's visible,
    attaching it to subsequent API calls. Now it's a trust boundary. A client-persisted id the server doesn't re-verify
    is impersonation-by-devtools, so this option pulls in a real token/session design, expiry, and logout. Expensive to
    retrofit and expensive to get wrong.

  My recommendation is tracking-only, because your stated goal is "track who logged in." But "other forms will need it
  later" is exactly how the second one arrives by accident — a form reads the stored id and starts trusting it — so I'd
  rather name it now than discover it in three commits.
